= Operating system =

== Overview ==
An '''operating system''' is system software that manages hardware resources and provides abstractions and services for programs and users.

Common responsibilities include processes, memory, files, storage, devices, scheduling, networking, protection, and isolation.

== Processes and execution ==
A program is stored code and data.

A process is an executing instance with runtime state, address space, resources, and scheduling context.

Therefore:

<pre>
program ≠ process
</pre>

== Memory management ==
Operating systems allocate memory, isolate address spaces, support virtual memory, map files, and coordinate shared memory.

Virtual memory separates a process's address view from immediate physical-memory placement.

== Files and storage ==
File systems organize persistent data into named structures with metadata, permissions, allocation, and recovery mechanisms.

Different operating systems support different filesystem and storage abstractions.

== Devices and I/O ==
Drivers mediate between generic OS interfaces and device-specific hardware.

Interrupts, DMA, buffering, queues, and asynchronous interfaces allow I/O to overlap computation.

== Protection ==
Operating systems enforce privilege boundaries, identities, permissions, process isolation, and resource controls.

These mechanisms support [[Computer security]] but do not by themselves guarantee security.

== Kernel and operating system ==
The kernel is the privileged core that directly manages critical resources.

An operating system usually includes much more: libraries, system services, utilities, boot components, management tools, and user-space infrastructure.

== Related concepts ==
[[Computer architecture]], [[Software]], [[Program (computing)]], and [[Computer security]] connect operating-system abstractions to the hardware/software boundary and protection mechanisms.

